2026-11-04 the author, of lrlr-parser, with Wikipedia's LALR parser, verbatim in part: "An LALR parser (look-ahead, left-to-right, rightmost derivation parser) is a type of parser for computer languages. It is a simplified version of a canonical LR parser. The LALR parser was invented by Frank DeRemer in his 1969 PhD dissertation, Practical Translators for LR(k) languages ... He showed that the LALR parser has more language recognition power than the LR(0) parser, while requiring the same number of states as the LR(0) parser ... It was also proven that there exist LR(1) languages that are not LALR ... The first algorithms for LALR parser generation were published in 1973. In 1982, DeRemer and Tom Pennello published an algorithm that generated highly memory-efficient LALR parsers. LALR parsers can be automatically generated from a grammar by an LALR parser generator such as Yacc or GNU Bison." So the author's "lrlr" is LALR, and the key document DeRemer's dissertation (1969). Beside the record: LALR merges the canonical LR(1) automaton's states that share a core (their items without the look-ahead), keeping the LR(0) number of states; that merging is a quotient, structure removed while the language recognised stays the same, except where the merge creates a reduce-reduce conflict (the LR(1) languages that are not LALR): the shape of the author's reduction question (seventy-three-forced: "What structure can be removed while the same canonical unfold / replay / projection still results?"), and the conflict is its falsifier. Yacc, the classic LALR generator, is Plan 9's own (its yacc) and Unix's; on this host both are installed, /usr/bin/yacc and /usr/bin/bison. Kept as the author's reading: the LALR parser as the engine for relating every node to its directives (repetition-problem).

